<h1>Traditional Attire of the Northeast</h1>

<section class="region-overview mb-2">
    <p>The traditional clothing of Northeast India is a vibrant testament to the region's rich cultural diversity and artistic heritage. Each tribe and community boasts unique weaves, patterns, colors, and styles, often reflecting their social status, clan affiliation, or specific ceremonial occasions. Handwoven textiles, intricate beadwork, and natural dyes are common features, showcasing incredible craftsmanship passed down through generations.</p>
    <p>Admire the beauty and significance of some iconic traditional garments:</p>
</section>

<hr class="mb-2">

<section class="dresses-showcase">
    <h2>A Kaleidoscope of Weaves</h2>

    <div class="tribe-grid">

        <div class="tribe-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/E8E8E8/969696?text=Mekhela+Chador" alt="Representation of Assamese Mekhela Chador">
            <div class="tribe-card-body">
                <h3>Mekhela Chador (Assam)</h3>
                <p>The elegant traditional attire for Assamese women, consisting of two main pieces: the 'Mekhela' (a wrap-around skirt) and the 'Chador' (a draped upper garment). Often woven from luxurious Muga, Pat, or Eri silk with intricate motifs.</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="tribe-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/D0D0D0/969696?text=Phanek+Inaphi" alt="Representation of Manipuri Phanek and Inaphi">
            <div class="tribe-card-body">
                <h3>Phanek & Inaphi (Manipur)</h3>
                <p>Worn by Meitei women, the 'Phanek' is a wrap-around skirt, often striped or plain, while the 'Inaphi' is a delicate, translucent shawl draped over the upper body. Designs vary, with specific patterns like the 'Moirang Phee' holding special significance.</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="tribe-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/E8E8E8/969696?text=Naga+Shawl" alt="Representation of a Naga Shawl">
            <div class="tribe-card-body">
                <h3>Naga Shawls (Nagaland)</h3>
                <p>Iconic symbols of Naga identity, these shawls vary significantly between tribes (Ao, Angami, Sema, Konyak etc.). Patterns, colors, and motifs often denote the wearer's tribe, clan, social status, or achievements. Woven with intricate detail, primarily by women.</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="tribe-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/D0D0D0/969696?text=Jainsem+Dhara" alt="Representation of Khasi Jainsem or Dhara">
            <div class="tribe-card-body">
                <h3>Jainsem / Dhara (Meghalaya)</h3>
                <p>The traditional dress for Khasi women. The 'Jainsem' is an elegant sleeveless dress worn over a blouse, often made of silk or cotton. The 'Dhara' is a more elaborate version, typically worn during festivals, featuring two pieces draped artistically.</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="tribe-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/E8E8E8/969696?text=Puanchei" alt="Representation of Mizo Puanchei">
            <div class="tribe-card-body">
                <h3>Puan (Mizoram)</h3>
                <p>'Puan' is the general term for Mizo traditional cloth. Specific types like 'Puanchei' (most colourful, worn on festive occasions), 'Ngotekherh' (originally for warriors), and 'Pawndum' showcase intricate weaving with distinct patterns and colours.</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="tribe-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/D0D0D0/969696?text=Gale+Galuk" alt="Representation of Arunachal Gale/Galuk">
            <div class="tribe-card-body">
                <h3>Gale / Galuk (Arunachal Pradesh)</h3>
                <p>Attire varies greatly among Arunachal's tribes. For example, Nyishi women wear a wrap-around skirt ('Gale') and upper garment, often adorned with beads, while men might wear sleeveless jackets ('Galuk') made of cotton or wool, along with distinct headgear.</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

         <div class="tribe-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/E8E8E8/969696?text=Rignai+Risa" alt="Representation of Tripuri Rignai and Risa">
            <div class="tribe-card-body">
                <h3>Rignai & Risa (Tripura)</h3>
                <p>Traditional attire for Tripuri women includes the 'Rignai', a lower garment wrapped around the waist, often with vibrant horizontal stripes, and the 'Risa', a smaller cloth covering the upper body, known for its intricate handwoven patterns.</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="tribe-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/D0D0D0/969696?text=Kho+Bakhu" alt="Representation of Sikkimese Kho/Bakhu">
            <div class="tribe-card-body">
                <h3>Kho / Bakhu (Sikkim)</h3>
                <p>A traditional loose, cloak-like garment worn by the Bhutia community (and influenced others like Lepchas). Men's 'Kho' is typically worn with trousers, while women's 'Bakhu' is a full-length dress fastened near the waist with a belt, often paired with a silk blouse.</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

